   "site_admin": false
  },
  "body": "`KafkaProducer.close(timeout)` can block forever. After the timed join, `close()` force closes the sender and then joins the I/O thread with no timeout. If a user callback is blocking that thread, for example while the forced close fails the pending records, `close()` waits for as long as the callback does, so the timeout is not honoured. `close(Duration.ZERO)` hangs the same way.\n\nSeparately, `Timer.remainingMs()` can return 0 and `Thread.join(0)` waits forever, so the first, timed join is unbounded too when the close timer has already run out.\n\nThis change:\n- caps the wait for the I/O thread after a forced close at `max.block.ms`, and logs a warning if the thread is still alive afterwards\n- never calls `Thread.join(0)`\n- documents the bound in the `close(Duration)` Javadoc\n\nThe forced shutdown normally takes milliseconds, so normal behaviour is unchanged: `close()` still waits for it, and the failed records' callbacks still run before `close()` returns. Only a blocked I/O thread now leads to a bounded wait instead of a hang. The new logic sits in two small helpers so that `close()` keeps its current structure.\n\nAn alternative is to bound the wait by the time left on the close timer, as the JIRA suggests. That keeps `close()` within its timeout, but whenever the timeout expires, callbacks and interceptors of the failed records would run after `close()` returns, while it closes the interceptors and metrics. Happy to switch if reviewers prefer that, or a different cap.\n\nTesting:\n- `testCloseWithTimeoutReturnsWhileCallbackIsBlocked` and `testCloseWithZeroTimeoutReturnsWhileCallbackIsBlocked`: a send callback blocks during the forced close, and `close(100 ms)` / `close(0)` must return. Both fail on trunk (`close()` is still blocked after 10 s) and pass with this change.\n- `testForcedCloseStillRunsCallbacksBeforeReturning`: guards the unchanged behaviour, and passes before and after.\n- All tests in `org.apache.kafka.clients.producer` pass locally (615 passed, 3 skipped; macOS, JDK 26), as do Checkstyle and SpotBugs for `clients`.\n\nThis complements #23857 (KAFKA-21132), which bounds the transactional abort during close.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
